   "text": {
    "L1": "Digestion changes complex food into simple, soluble substances the body can absorb and use.",
    "L2": "It begins in the mouth. Teeth make small pieces, and saliva starts changing starch into sugar.",
    "L3": "That is why roti chewed for a long time can taste sweet. The food pipe then pushes food down in wave-like squeezes.",
    "L4": "In the stomach, food is churned and mixed with acid and digestive juices. Protein digestion begins there, while mucus protects the stomach wall.",
    "L5": "So the idea that all digestion happens in the stomach is not true. Much more work continues after it.",
    "L6": "Most digestion is completed in the small intestine with juices from its walls, bile, and pancreatic juice.",
    "L7": "Finger-like villi absorb the digested food into the blood. The large intestine absorbs water and some salts from what remains.",
    "L8": "Waste is stored in the rectum and passed out through the anus. Now put the parts in the order food travels."
   },
